The error log indicates a CPU exception on a RISC-V system, specifically showing a register dump that includes `s2` through `s11`. However, the Device Tree Source (DTS) file `dts/riscv/qemu/virt-riscv32e.dtsi` specifies `riscv,isa-base = "rv32e"`, which means the target architecture is RISC-V RV32E. The RV32E specification defines a reduced register set, where only `s0` and `s1` are callee-saved registers, and `s2` through `s11` do not exist.

The provided RISC-V source files (`arch/riscv/core/coredump.c`, `arch/riscv/core/fatal.c`, `include/zephyr/arch/riscv/thread.h`, `arch/riscv/core/offsets/offsets.c`) correctly use conditional compilation based on `CONFIG_RISCV_ISA_RV32E` to adapt to the RV32E ISA. The fact that `s2` through `s11` are being printed in the error log implies that `CONFIG_RISCV_ISA_RV32E` was *not* defined during the compilation of `arch/riscv/core/fatal.c`. This indicates a mismatch between the intended RV32E target (as per DTS) and the Kconfig configuration used during compilation.

To fix this, instead of relying on the `CONFIG_RISCV_ISA_RV32E` Kconfig option, which appears to be misconfigured or not propagated correctly, we can use the GCC/Clang predefined macro `__riscv_isa_ext_e`. This macro is automatically defined by the compiler when the 'E' extension is enabled (e.g., via the `-march=rv32e` compiler flag, which should be derived from the DTS). By switching to `__riscv_isa_ext_e`, the code will correctly adapt to the RV32E ISA at compile time, regardless of the `CONFIG_RISCV_ISA_RV32E` Kconfig setting.

The `arch/mips/core/offsets/offsets.c` file is irrelevant to this RISC-V issue and will not be modified.

